        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">4. Public vs. Private IPs: What Is My IP and IP Lookup</h2>
        <p>
          Devices on your local Wi-Fi or Ethernet network receive private RFC 1918 addresses (such as <code>10.0.0.0/8</code>, <code>172.16.0.0/12</code>, or <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>). Those addresses are non-routable on the public internet. Your router uses <strong>Network Address Translation (NAT)</strong> to map your local traffic onto the single public IP address assigned by your ISP.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two tools make verifying public addressing effortless:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/network/my-ip/"><strong>My IP Checker:</strong></a> Instantly reveals your public IPv4 or IPv6 address, ISP, timezone, and approximate city—essential when whitelisting your IP in AWS security groups, SSH firewalls, or database access lists.</li>
          <li><a href="/network/ip-lookup/"><strong>IP Address Lookup:</strong></a> Inspects any third-party public IP found in Nginx, Cloudflare, or SSH auth logs to identify its Autonomous System Number (ASN), hosting provider, country, and region.</li>
        </ul>

